{
  "dom_id": "step-q143483",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ people is drawn from a population. The population has people of two different weights, $78\\,\\text{kg}$ and $90\\,\\text{kg},$ distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$",
  "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the people in the population have weights $78\\,\\text{kg}$ and $90\\,\\text{kg},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+1=3$ people only with the following weights:\n\n$$\n78\\,\\text{kg},\\,78\\,\\text{kg},\\,90\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 78+90) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(156+90) \\\\  & =\\frac{246}{3} \\\\  & =82\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot {78}^{2}+{90}^{2})-{82}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(20\\,268)-6\\,724 \\\\  & =6\\,756-6\\,724 \\\\  & =32\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{32},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{n}} & <0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{32}}{0.1} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{{0.1}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3\\,200} \\\\ n & >3\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=3\\,201.$",
